 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public authorities law, in relation to the liability of a\nmunicipality or governmental subdivision for the refund of property taxes to\nthe Long Island power authority",
    "basePrintNo": "S1313",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S1313-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 9,
       "fullName": "Todd Kaminsky",
       "imgName": "903_todd_kaminsky.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 918,
       "sessionMemberId": 1816,
       "sessionYear": 2021,
       "shortName": "KAMINSKY"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1020-q, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S1313\n\nSPONSOR: GAUGHRAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public authorities law, in relation to the liability\nof a municipality or governmental subdivision for the refund of property\ntaxes to the Long Island power authority\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to limit the financial impact of adverse tax\ncertiorari challenges by LIPA on affected local governmental entities.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one adds a new subdivision four to section 1020-q of the public\nauthorities law. Parallel to the preceding subdivision, this provision\nwould limit the remedies available to LIPA when bringing tax certiorari\nchallenges by prohibiting relief in the form of repayment of all or part\nof the taxes assessed against any power plant, transmission line or\nsubstation from a prior year. This limitation would be extended to third\nparty power producers receiving reimbursement for property taxes by the\nAuthority.\n\nSection two contains a severability clause.\n\nSection three provides that this act shall take effect thirty days after\nbecoming law.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 1998, LIPA acquired all the capital stock and associated assets of\nLong Island Lighting Company (LILCO), which operated the electrical\nsystem on Long Island. In connection with that acquisition, the authori-\nty took over ultimate responsibility for providing electric utility\nservice to residential, commercial, industrial, nonprofit and govern-\nmental customers in the counties of Suffolk, Nassau and a portion of\nQueens.\n\nAt the time of acquisition, then-Chairman Richard Kessel memorialized an\nagreement that LIPA would never challenge the assessed values of their\nproperties, provided the municipalities never raised them. The devastat-\ning impact that tax-certiorari challenges would have on local govern-\nmental entities was recognized from LIPA's inception.\n\nIn fact, at the time of acquisition the Shoreham Power Plant (through\nLILCO), was challenging its tax assessment. To remedy this, a provision\nwas added to the enabling law governing LIPA providing that no refund of\ntaxes resulting from any judicial determination would be collected from\nthe impacted municipality (see Public Authorities Law 1020-q(3)).\n\nToday, LIPA has initiated tax certiorari lawsuits across Long Island in\nrelation to various properties owned by the Authority. They are chal-\nlenging their assessments for the current year, and seeking.  ,relief in\nthe form of a refund for taxes paid in prior years. This relief, if\ngranted, would be detrimental to the vitality of the communities in\nwhich these properties are located and would have a direct impact on\ntheir ability to provide indispensable programs and services. These\nactions are in direct conflict with LIPA's enabling statute which\nexplicitly provides that the establishment of the authority will, among\nother priorities, realize savings for the . . .  taxpayers in the\nservice area (see Public Authorities Law 1020-a).\n\nThis legislation would prevent LIPA for seeking relief in the form of a\nreturn of past year tax payments, thereby limiting the financial hard-\nship that local governments would endure in the event of an adverse\njudgment. This limitation would also be extended to third party power\nproducers that are reimbursed by LIPA for property taxes.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019-20: S5122A/A7786\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the state.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
